>> who else depends on context.  But, since this is a first person pronoun, 
>> those others cusku this as well (definitional).
>>
>
> How can they cusku if they are not  in this dialog? English "we" covers 
> {mi jo'u mibdrata}.
>
>
>> Sent from my iPad
>>
>> On Jun 20, 2013, at 7:48, la arxokuna <gleki.is...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>
>>
>>
>> On Thursday, June 20, 2013 4:39:08 PM UTC+4, clifford wrote:
>>>
>>> So, {lo za'u cusku be dei }.  {no'u} just sounds wrong;it is not an 
>>> identity, merely a predication, probably {poi}.  For the rest it is over 
>>> specification in that these other things may or may not be included.  {lo} 
>>> does it's usual selection job in context.  In particular, lo na'e cusku are 
>>> not to be included, by the nature of the gadri. 
>>>
>>
>> Don't you think that your definition contradicts Wiktionary that says 
>> that "we=the speaker and at least one other person" ? It is not said that 
>> that other persons are to be viewed as speakers.
>>
>>
